[s0l0!-header][h1]String Input[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Programs are often interactive, asking the user for input.
To retrieve a line of text or other string from the user, C provides the [b]scanf[/b](), [b]gets[/b](), and [b]fgets[/b]() functions.
You can use [b]scanf[/b]() to read input according to the format specifiers.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1743"]char first_name[25];
int age;
printf("Enter your first name and age: \n");
[b]scanf[/b]("%s %d", first_name, &age); [/code]
When [b]scanf[/b]() is used to read a string, there is no need for [b]&[/b] to access the variable address because an array name acts as a pointer.
[b]scanf[/b]() stops reading input when it reaches a space. To read a string with spaces, use the [b]gets()[/b] function. It reads input until a terminating newline is reached (the Enter key is pressed).
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1759"]char full_name[50];
printf("Enter your full name: ");
[b]gets[/b](full_name);[/code]
A safer alternative to [b]gets[/b]() is [b]fgets()[/b], which reads up to a specified number of characters. This approach helps prevent a [b]buffer overflow[/b], which happens when the string array isn't big enough for the typed text. 
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1760"]char full_name[50];
printf("Enter your full name: ");
[b]fgets[/b](full_name, 50, stdin);[/code]
The [b]fgets[/b]() arguments are the string name, the number of characters to read, and a pointer to where you want to read the string from. [b]stdin[/b] means to read from the [b]standard input[/b], which is the keyboard.
Another difference between [b]gets [/b]and [b]fgets [/b]is that the newline character is stored by [b]fgets[/b].
[note][b]fgets[/b]() reads only [b]n-1[/b] characters from [b]stdin [/b]because there must be room for '[b]\0[/b]'.[/note]

