[s0l0!-header][h1]String Output[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

String output is handled with the [b]fputs[/b](), [b]puts[/b](), and [b]printf[/b]() functions.
The [b]fputs()[/b] requires the name of the string and a pointer to where you want to print the string. To print to the screen, use [b]stdout[/b] which refers to the [b]standard output[/b]. 
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1744"]#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
  char city[40];
  printf("Enter your favorite city: ");
  gets(city);
  // Note: for safety, use
  // fgets(city, 40, stdin);

  [b]fputs[/b](city, stdout);
  printf(" is a fun city.");

  return 0;
}[/code]
The [b]puts()[/b] function takes only a string argument and can also be used to display output. However, it adds a newline to output.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1745"]#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
  char city[40];
  printf("Enter your favorite city: ");
  gets(city);
  // Note: for safety, use
  // fgets(city, 40, stdin);

  [b]puts[/b](city);

  return 0;
}[/code]
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

